{"id": "aic-12909", "status": "ok", "meta": "A felled tree lies before the fire-damaged Kostverloren castle in Rembrandt's drawing from about 1652, the estate nicknamed the money pit.", "about": "A felled tree lies across the foreground, and behind it stand the worn buildings of Kostverloren castle. The name means lost expenses, or, less formally, money pit, and the museum says Rembrandt's circle was drawn to the estate because its owners kept running into bad luck. A fire destroyed much of the castle. Rembrandt walked onto the grounds after the damage and before the repairs, and drew what was left. The museum thinks the tree's hard horizontal line sets off the faded grandeur of the buildings.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/12909", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "10.9 × 17.5 cm (4 5/16 × 6 15/16 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/12909", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-12909"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "aic-8991", "status": "ok", "meta": "Kandinsky's Improvisation No. 30 (Cannons), an abstract canvas of 1913 that he said was not really about war.", "about": "The cannons in the title are not what Kandinsky wanted people to look for. He wrote to the Chicago collector Arthur Jerome Eddy that they could probably be explained by the constant war talk of that year, but that the true content is what viewers feel under the effect of the forms and colors. Between 1910 and 1914 he painted a series of improvisations, which he described as unconscious, spontaneous expressions. His 1912 book Concerning the Spiritual in Art compares painting to music.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/8991", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "111 × 111.3 cm (43 11/16 × 43 13/16 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/8991", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-8991"], "check": "clean"}
